#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199355} :activity/id #uuid "d1c39739-73bf-5e10-924b-60ad2298b6b6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049006 :topic/id #uuid "d2c54a4b-c92e-55a9-bee8-9cf670399fdb" :topic/math-academy-id 4198} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199494 :tutorial/content "**Measuring** means finding a number that describes the size of something. The most popular measurement system within the scientific community is the **metric system**.\n\nWe determine how much space an object occupies by measuring its **volume**. We use **volume units** to specify an object's volume.\n\nA common metric unit of volume is the **liter,** which we denote using the symbol \"$\\textrm{L}$.\" We can use **measuring jugs** like the ones below to measure the volume of a liquid.\n\n![](images/fa/fa15e2d9b446412876732435aa06b8117fbb4903ab179e4cc64c50da4868fee7.png)\n\nWe use **milliliters** to measure smaller volumes. The symbol for milliliters is \"$\\textrm{mL}$.\" The prefix $\\textrm m$ stands for **milli,** which means\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,\\textrm{mL}$ equals\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\textrm L\n$$\n\nNotice that if we add $1\\,\\textrm{mL}$ to itself $1\\,000$ times, we get exactly $1\\,\\textrm{L}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1mL + 1mL + ⋯ + 1mL^(⏞)^(1000times) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{1000}L + \\frac{1}{1000}L + ⋯ + \\frac{1}{1000}L &=  \\\\\n1000 \\cdot \\frac{1}{1000}L &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1000 \\cdot 1}{1000}L &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1000}{1000}L &=  \\\\\n&= 1L\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mL}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{L}$, as stated in the diagram." :tutorial/id #uuid "134c077c-df22-5aea-bfa9-f7c4716a73cc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7791 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c32907f2-233a-5aa3-987b-3822861623cd" :step/math-academy-id 45091 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199356}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199356 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198424 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198066 :question/id #uuid "c0546e5d-93b8-5ccd-9833-c5d1cfc1533a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12512"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b8161e6-678c-5fb6-b1bd-3e3d78913495" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198668}] :knowledge-point/title "Metric Units of Volume"} :step/id #uuid "fb4a9756-596a-54cc-983a-27b78164ef81" :step/math-academy-id 45092 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199379}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199357 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198425 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198067 :question/id #uuid "fb4a9b9a-9e34-5d0d-9be0-02627b2058b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12513"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5957039e-4732-518a-b8e3-ca00900e3a25" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198674}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Customary Units of Volume"} :step/id #uuid "174667d5-856c-58b3-b6cd-91535b19904a" :step/math-academy-id 45093 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199361}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199361 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198429 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198071 :question/id #uuid "490ae71b-5dbf-5315-9a00-2dda8677c47b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12521"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2630641a-ee5f-5a10-a67d-aea77af4471c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198675}] :knowledge-point/title "Stating Volume Conversions in Customary Units"} :step/id #uuid "94fdf204-2c0b-58d3-ae16-72dd716cda7f" :step/math-academy-id 45101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199379 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199505 :tutorial/content "Another way to measure volume is to use **customary units.** The most common customary units for volume are **fluid ounce**, **cup**, **pint**, and **gallon**.\n\nA table showing the customary units of volume is given below.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Gallon | $\\textrm{gal}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{gal} = 8\\,\\textrm{pints}$ |\n| Pint | $\\textrm{pt}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{pt} = 2\\,\\textrm{cups}$ |\n| Cup | $-$ | $1\\,\\textrm{cup} = 8\\,\\textrm{fl oz}$ |\n| Fluid ounce | $\\textrm{fl oz}$ | $-$ |\n\nNote that \"gallons\" is the largest volume unit, and \"fluid ounces\" is the smallest.\n\nCustomary units are not standard in the scientific community. However, they are commonly used in the United States and other countries." :tutorial/id #uuid "8b85c4f9-8e08-561e-b532-57b435d9464f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7874 :tutorial/title "Customary Units of Volume"} :step/id #uuid "8414405b-f57b-5014-8866-4c48ceb09fee" :step/math-academy-id 45409 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199357}}] :activity/title "Units of Volume" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198503}]}
